WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.252
The examples in this module use a small synthetic healthcare database.

00:00:04.252 --> 00:00:07.537
We have a patients table with one row per patient.

00:00:07.537 --> 00:00:13.052
Then we have child tables such as admissions, lab results, and prescriptions.

00:00:13.052 --> 00:00:18.927
A single patient can have no admissions, one admission, or several admissions.

00:00:18.927 --> 00:00:23.057
The same idea applies to lab tests and prescriptions.

00:00:23.057 --> 00:00:29.242
We also have departments and medical staff, and admissions can reference an attending staff member.

00:00:29.242 --> 00:00:33.332
This gives us several realistic paths for joining tables.

00:00:33.332 --> 00:00:36.677
The dataset intentionally includes edge cases.

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:42.222
For example, one patient may have no related activity, and the staging data may include an ID that does not

00:00:42.222 --> 00:00:45.847
exist in the official patient table.

00:00:45.847 --> 00:00:52.012
Those are not random complications; they represent the kinds of imperfect relationships analysts regularly

00:00:52.012 --> 00:00:54.732
encounter in operational data.

00:00:54.732 --> 00:01:00.947
Those edge cases are important because they let us see what different join types actually preserve.

00:01:00.947 --> 00:01:06.612
In a real project, unmatched records are often the records you care about most.

00:01:06.612 --> 00:01:10.787
A bank might want to find transactions with no matching customer.

00:01:10.787 --> 00:01:15.147
A university might want to find students with no registration record.

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:19.127
A company might want to find products that have never been sold.

00:01:19.127 --> 00:01:23.412
As you read the queries, keep the relationships in mind.

00:01:23.412 --> 00:01:30.377
Joining patients to admissions changes the result from patient-level data to admission-level detail.

00:01:30.377 --> 00:01:35.997
If a patient has three admissions, that patient information may repeat three times.

00:01:35.997 --> 00:01:39.772
That repetition is not automatically a duplicate problem.

00:01:39.772 --> 00:01:42.427
It reflects the structure of the data.

00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:48.522
Later, when we build department metrics and patient summaries, you will see how the same source tables can be

00:01:48.522 --> 00:01:53.272
transformed to a different grain depending on the analytical question.
